> Write a compact Python function that reads one note as UTF-8 and returns a count of normalized words. Explain its treatment of punctuation and Unicode in at most 100 words.
[0m```python
import markdown
import re

def count_normalized_words(filepath):
    with open(filepath, 'r', encoding='utf-8') as f:
        text = markdown.markdown(f.read())
    words = re.findall(r'\b\w+\b', text.lower()) # Find word boundaries
    return len(words)
```

This function reads a Markdown note, converts it to plain text, and uses `re.findall` to extract words.  It lowercases the text and removes punctuation via the regex `\b\w+\b`. Unicode characters are handled correctly by the UTF-8 encoding and the `\w+` regex (which matches Unicode word characters).
